\chapter{Disks: virtio and the SD Card}
\label{ch:disks}

The block layer of the book (buffer cache, Minix file system) sits on top of one function that reads and writes blocks. Two
drivers sit below it. \texttt{virtio.c} drives qemu's virtual disks (and is the model of how a driver wakes a sleeping process from
an interrupt). \texttt{sdcard.c} drives the SD card of the Raspberry Pi through its SDHCI controller; following the book's
principle of \emph{simple first, then better} it began with polling and now handles multi-block commands, four data lines and
interrupts. The partition table on the card tells which Minix partitions are the disks \texttt{hda}, \texttt{hdb} and \texttt{fd1}.

@
\section{The disks of the system: virtio-mmio block devices (polling or interrupts), or partitions of an SD card}
\label{src:virtio.c}

Replaces the [[x86]] IDE (PIO) and floppy (DMA) drivers: the same read/write-sectors
interface, but the ``disk'' is a virtio-blk device on the qemu ``virt'' machine.
Supports the legacy (version 1) and the modern (version 2) MMIO transport. On a board without virtio devices (the
Raspberry Pi) the disks are the Ulix partitions of the SD card ([[sdcard.c]]); they are used through the same functions.

@

The complete file is put together from these parts:

