\section{Reading many blocks with one request (issue \#140)}
\label{src:fastfs.c}

\subsection*{What was slow, and why}

Starting the Python interpreter took one to three minutes under qemu. The first guess, ``the disk is slow'', was wrong in an
interesting way: the file is only 7.4~MByte, and the book's \texttt{read ()} path moves a block in half a millisecond. What
the measurement of the first step showed was this. The kernel loads a program with its own small Minix reader
(\texttt{minixfs.c}), one KByte at a time, and for every KByte it

\begin{itemize}
\item read the inode again (a block of the inode table),
\item and, for a file in the double indirect range (everything above 263 KByte), read the double indirect zone, the indirect
      zone below it and then the data zone,
\end{itemize}

all through the buffer cache of the book, which has 512 blocks and cannot hold what a 7~MByte program pushes through it. That
is up to four disk requests for each KByte: 13\,881 requests for the interpreter, about two per KByte on the average.
Every request means a trip through the whole interrupt path: the process goes to sleep in \texttt{harddisk\_queue}, the
device interrupt wakes it, the scheduler runs it again --- in a loaded system several timer ticks after the data had arrived.
That adds up to 60 to 130 seconds of the start. The fix has two halves that are independent of each other, and the second
one only works because of the first.

\subsection*{Half one: remember what was read}

\texttt{minix\_read} keeps a small \texttt{struct reader} for the length of one call: the inode and the last indirect zones
it has read, each with its zone number. The first KByte of a file costs an inode and perhaps two indirect zones; each of the next 255
KByte costs nothing for the lookup. (The structure is a local variable of the function: the earlier bug with static buffers
that a sleeping reader and a second thread overwrote, section 30 of \texttt{ARM64-PORT.md}, is the reason why nothing here is
shared or kept between two calls.)

\subsection*{Half two: one request for a run of zones}

A file that was written to an empty disk by one process has consecutive zone numbers: zone $n$, $n+1$, $n+2$ and so on. With
the zone numbers at hand, \texttt{minix\_read} looks at how far this holds and asks for the whole run at once, at most 64
zones (64 KByte). The driver below already took more than one sector per request --- virtio-blk's request is a header, one
data buffer of any length and a status byte, and the SD controller has the multi-block commands 18 and 25 --- so no new
descriptor chain was needed; the only thing missing was a caller that asked for more than a block. That is \texttt{readblocks}
(in \texttt{glue.c}), next to \texttt{readblock}, which the book's buffer cache and all its callers keep using:

\begin{itemize}
\item the data lands in a buffer of the kernel, \texttt{run\_buffer}, and is copied from there: a device moves its data to
      \emph{physically contiguous} memory, which a user buffer or a stack is not, and the frames into which the program loader
      copies are scattered all over RAM;
\item the single buffer is protected by a lock, because the reader sleeps on the interrupt, and the thread that is scheduled
      meanwhile may want to read, too;
\item the buffer cache is not filled (7 MByte would push everything else out of it) but it is looked at: a block that the cache
      holds may be newer than the disk, so it is taken from the cache after the transfer;
\item if the request fails, the blocks are read one at a time with \texttt{readblock}, as before. Slower, and correct.
\end{itemize}

The program loader asks for one page at a time, for frames that are not next to each other, so \texttt{elf.c} keeps a window of 64
KByte on the file: one call for the window, and the next 15 pages are copies from it.

\subsection*{The path of a user program}

The book's \texttt{read ()} goes through \texttt{mx\_read}, which does the same one block at a time (it keeps its inode in memory, but
looks up the zone of every block again). A Python import reads a source file with one \texttt{read} of the file's size; that
call should not become a hundred requests either. So the wrapper chain that \texttt{pipes.c} puts around the book's \texttt{u\_read}
sends a read of two or more blocks on a regular file of the root disk, opened for reading, to \texttt{minix\_read\_fast}. The
conditions are those under which the in-memory inode and the disk agree: the file is open read-only, the book's inode is
\emph{clean} (it has not been changed since it was read), and the disk is the root disk, which \texttt{minixfs.c} serves. In
every other case the function says ``not for me'' and the book does what it always did.

<<fastfs.c>>=
#define hash fastfs_hash                           // (the book's header defines a function of this name: keep it out of the link)
#include "ulix-api.h"
#undef hash

extern long minix_read (unsigned ino_nr, unsigned offset, void *buf, unsigned len);       // minixfs.c

// a read () of a regular file of the root disk that is longer than a block, by minix_read (one request for a run of zones);
// -2: this call is not for the fast path, the book's mx_read does it
int minix_read_fast (int gfd, void *buf, int nbyte) {
  if (gfd < 0 || (gfd >> 8) != FS_MINIX || nbyte < 2 * BLOCK_SIZE) return -2;
  struct mx_filestat *st = &mx_status[gfd & 0xff];
  struct int_minix2_inode *in = st->int_inode;
  if (in == NULL || st->mode != O_RDONLY) return -2;                      // (a file that is written is the book's business)
  if (!in->clean || in->device != DEV_HDA || (in->i_mode & S_IFMT) != S_IFREG) return -2;
  if (st->pos < 0 || st->pos >= (int) in->i_size) return 0;               // end of file
  if (nbyte > (int) in->i_size - st->pos) nbyte = in->i_size - st->pos;
  long got = minix_read (in->ino, st->pos, buf, nbyte);
  if (got < 0) return -2;                                                 // let the book try and report the error
  st->pos += got;
  return got;
}
